{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-276446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $y=8x-1$ intersects the curve $y=\\frac{1}{2x}-1$ at the points $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $B.$\n\nThe coordinates of $B$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=8x-1 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2x}-1 \\\\ 8x & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =-2-1 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x-1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})-1 \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $A(-\\frac{1}{4},-3)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/bb69eb0150879f215fe453e596ae18273ec149f1d1486c8d7dfe7b2c1e3e5e58.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-276446" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276446" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276446" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276446" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "**From left to right,** the points of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x-1}+2$ and the line $y-x-1=0$ are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$),\\,B($\n{{field-3}},\n{{field-4}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{4}{x-1}+2 \\\\ y=x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-1}+2 & =x+1 \\\\ 4+2(x-1) & =(x+1)(x-1) \\\\ 2+2x & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =(-1)-1 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,0).$\n\n- Substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+1 \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(3,4).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/6166b8bbae6ae1e528a72a5533e0e6906547e7e6076790cdb23306d11bfe9063.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276450" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ intersects the line $y=2x$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}-1 \\\\ y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}-1 & =2x \\\\ \\frac{1-x}{x} & =2x \\\\ 1-x & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,-2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$ \n\nFinally, if we let $p=-1,q=-2,r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $s=1,$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-1-2+\\frac{1}{2}+1=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/0a2420b1e390edd61efd28a223cd1beb93db98609a8d31c69526434e7188658c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-124047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-3471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y-5x=8$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-3471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-3471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-3471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-3471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-3471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "**From left to right,** the points of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}-5$ and the line $y=8x+1$ are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}\n$),\\,B($\n{{field-3}},\n{{field-4}}\n$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{x}-5 \\\\ y=8x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-5 & =8x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =8x+6 \\\\ 2 & =8{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+6x-2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =0 \\\\ (4x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4},-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=-1.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8x+1 \\\\  & =8(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-8+1 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(-1,-7).$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the points of intersection are $A(-1,-7)$ and $B(\\frac{1}{4},3).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/5432e52f6099ec6e2d72ea6031e303eeaa0043129eea40baa064dd59b0d46520.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276447" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-41957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["text" "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect."] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{2x+3}$ and the line $y=1.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{2}{2x+3} \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2x+3} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =2x+3 \\\\ -1 & =2x \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/192f47788ac2bc716c4f3e0d8fa9efe9b2c26dd9e32bb9296cfcb00328e5db05.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-277386" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277386" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277386" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277386" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2$ and the line $y=4.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3x-2} & =2 \\\\ 5 & =6x-4 \\\\ 9 & =6x \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(\\frac{3}{2},4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/f07fc53462af6fbb7de0a901ae23721a2eb84f6e83bd5577a2659c175519fc67.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-277383" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277383" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277383" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277383" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the line $y=-x+5$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+5 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+5 & =\\frac{2}{x-5}+2 \\\\ -x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-5} \\\\ (-x+3)(x-5) & =2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+17 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-8)\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4(1)(17)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{64-68}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-x+5$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-5}+2.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/73570088dd6c424f4848b25ce3305edaf1ec50c07039b463f4af9a5dd35bfb43.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-70463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["text" "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the line $y=-2x-1$ and the curve $y=\\frac{3}{x+3}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x-1 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{x+3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-1 & =\\frac{3}{x+3}+1 \\\\ -2x-2 & =\\frac{3}{x+3} \\\\ (-2x-2)(x+3) & =3 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}-8x-6 & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4(2)(9)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64-72}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{-8}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x-1$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{3}{x+3}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/cae5448d236e7e07bd2edf2281f495703b90b2cb3a160bd3d1a18e5915a8abaf.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-70457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["text" "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-70457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$ for $k\\ne 0,$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ 2x(x-2) & =k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(2)(-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-124166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the line $y=4x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{2}{x+1},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=4x-k \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-k & =-\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ (4x-k)(x+1) & =-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+4x-kx-k+2 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+(4-k)x+(2-k) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(4-k)\\pm \\sqrt{(4-k)^{2}-4(4)(2-k)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{16-8k+{k}^{2}-32+16k}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-4)\\pm \\sqrt{{k}^{2}+8k-16}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+8k-16 & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{(8)^{2}-4(1)(-16)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm 8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $k=-4+4\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-277432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-2-2\\sqrt{2}$ or $-2+2\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $-4+4\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $4\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$-8-8\\sqrt{2}$ or $-8+8\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$-\\sqrt{2}$ or $\\sqrt{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-277432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-4-4\\sqrt{2}$ or $-4+4\\sqrt{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $y=4x-5$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1$, where $k\\ne -5,$  only once. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the line $y=4x-5$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1$,  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:      \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=4x-5 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k+5}{x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-5 & =\\frac{k+5}{x}+1 \\\\ 4x-6 & =\\frac{k+5}{x} \\\\ (4x-6)x & =k+5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x-k-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4(4)(-k-5)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{36+16k+80}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{116+16k}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}116+16k & =0 \\\\ 16k & =-116 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{116}{16} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{29\\cdot 4}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $k$ is $-\\frac{29}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-276454" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-276454" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{29}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ where $k\\ne 0,$ twice. What is the range of possible values of  $k?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-k &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-k & =\\frac{k}{x}-k \\\\ x-k & =\\frac{k-kx}{x} \\\\ x(x-k) & =k-kx \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola at two points, the quadratic formula above must give two real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be positive: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k\\in (0,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-124169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k\\in (-\\infty ,0)"] ["math" "k\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )"] ["math" "k\\in (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )"] ["math" "k\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )"] ["math" "k\\in (0,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-124169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k\\in (0,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$, where $k\\ne -1$, only once. What is the value of $k?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$,  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+4 &  \\\\ y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1 \\\\ 2x+3 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x} \\\\ (2x+3)x & =-(k+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3x+k+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(2)(k+1)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-8k-8}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{1-8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=\\frac{1}{8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8a3058a31a4cdd0d52561895d53ddb5ab32eea5c0046cbbb8ffd640eab3f43c"} {"question" "q-41970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "q-41970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/state.json" "file_sha256" "21b1966ad7a57af18855579e9eee3d966f6a3a7292b3d9d28a112835f451a790"} {"question" "e-5193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/76c0e8aed74f2dcf3a382f79768098eda4f359631cdffb812a0aeccbb9707976.png)\n\nFind the points where the line $y=2x-3$ and the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}$ intersect (shown as the points $A$ and $B$ above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/example-5193.json" "file_sha256" "89f4c067b86668408854ad4f532a01a7bef7763800385ad205aeba9641e03838"} {"question" "e-5193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ (x-2)(2x-3) & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(2x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(1)-3 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1).$\n\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/example-5193.json" "file_sha256" "89f4c067b86668408854ad4f532a01a7bef7763800385ad205aeba9641e03838"} {"question" "e-5192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/example-5192.json" "file_sha256" "f5b1f36df1b444b502c86b6708e987e3582210acd0217eca113904b90c87240e"} {"question" "e-5192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 \\\\ y=-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 & =-x+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-2} & =-x+2 \\\\ 1 & =(-x+2)(x-2) \\\\ 1 & =-{x}^{2}+4x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16-20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/30eb7faffa9cdfbffedbbf0c9d48408fb1be1b8101da80864c4bced5d53d9903.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/example-5192.json" "file_sha256" "f5b1f36df1b444b502c86b6708e987e3582210acd0217eca113904b90c87240e"} {"question" "e-5260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/example-5260.json" "file_sha256" "5ddfb207ff33d37fbb15b585029b1b10bf56f19396510655cc0cdf86cf1bbdd7"} {"question" "e-5260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the line $y=2x+3$ intersects the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3},$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{k}{x+3} \\\\ y=2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x+3} & =2x+3 \\\\ k & =(2x+3)(x+3) \\\\ k & =2{x}^{2}+9x+9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+9x+9-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{{9}^{2}-4(2)(9-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{81-72+8k}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{9+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-9 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\dfrac{9}{8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/example-5260.json" "file_sha256" "5ddfb207ff33d37fbb15b585029b1b10bf56f19396510655cc0cdf86cf1bbdd7"}] "usage" {"q-3471" [] "e-5192" [] "e-5193" [] "q-41970" [] "e-5260" [] "q-41957" []} "basis" 2207 "retractions" [[17592186069194 :question/problem "The curve $y = \\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y - 5x = 8$ at the points $(p, q)$ and $(r, s)$. What is $p + q + r + s$?"] [17592186069256 :question/problem "If the line $y =-2x + 3$ and the curve $y = \\frac{2}{x - 2} + 1$ intersect at the point $(p, q)$, then $p + q =$"] [17592186069258 :question/problem "The line $y = 2x + 4$ intersects the hyperbola $y =-\\frac{k + 1}{x} + 1$, where $k \\ne - 1$, only once. What is the value of $k$?"] [17592186068526 :question/problem "Find the points where the line $y=2x-3$ and the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}$ intersect (shown as the points $A$ and $B$ above).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/314995c0a1ee3ffb68009d42ba923120.png)"] [17592186068526 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = 2x - 3; y = \\frac{1}{x - 2}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3 &= \\frac{1}{x - 2} \\\\\n(x - 2)(2x - 3) &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 6 &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1, \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(1) - 3; =-1$,\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1)$.\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(\\frac{5}{2}) - 3; = 2$,\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)\n$$"] [17592186068525 :question/problem "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3$."] [17592186068525 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1; y =-x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1 &= -x + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} &= -x + 2 \\\\\n1 &= (-x + 2)(x - 2) \\\\\n1 &= -x^{2} + 4x - 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (4 ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 - 20}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+3\n$$\n\ndoes not intersect the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/c1d0bf837c05cf29a2460425213cd5d1.png)"] [17592186068530 :question/problem "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k$?"] [17592186068530 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nintersects the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{k}{x + 3}; y = 2x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k}{x + 3} &= 2x + 3 \\\\\nk &= (2x + 3)(x + 3) \\\\\nk &= 2x^{2} + 9x + 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 9x + 9 - k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-9 ± \\sqrt{9^{2} - 4(2)(9 - k)})/(2(2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{81 - 72 + 8k}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{9 + 8k}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 + 8k & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < - 9 \\\\ k & < - \\frac{9}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is\n\n$$\nk<-\\dfrac{9}{8}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186066696 17592186066697 17592186066698 17592186066699 17592186066700 17592186067068 17592186067069 17592186067070 17592186067071 17592186067072 17592186067080 17592186067081 17592186067082 17592186067083 17592186067084] "immutable_fields" [[17592186066695 :answer-field/correct] [17592186066695 :answer-field/id] [17592186066695 :answer-field/key] [17592186066695 :answer-field/type] [17592186067067 :answer-field/correct] [17592186067067 :answer-field/id] [17592186067067 :answer-field/key] [17592186067067 :answer-field/type] [17592186067079 :answer-field/correct] [17592186067079 :answer-field/id] [17592186067079 :answer-field/key] [17592186067079 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
